    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "60148d39_4119f5a4",
        "filename": "drivers/staging/android/virt_wifi.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291337
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-14T21:19:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve described the scenarios I\u0027ve been able to think of below for this code, is there a case missing?\n\n- Multiple invocations of virt_wifi_scan_result: impossible. virt_wifi_scan is guarded by the wireless_dev mutex, so there is only ever one caller inside that method. That method is the only place that can schedule virt_wifi_scan_result calls, and it guards itself against doing that by checking if priv-\u003escan_request is null. This requires setting priv-\u003escan_request to NULL at the last possible moment to avoid overlapping runs.\n- wiphy / net_device is taken down: state is protected by virt_wifi_cancel_scan, which is invoked by the network going down callback. It uses cancel_delayed_work_sync which either waits for the scheduled callback to finish if it is already executing, or unschedules the callback if it has not started executing. That leaves it in a definite not-executing state when virt_wifi_cancel_scan interacts with priv-\u003escan_request.\n- Deletion of the net_device / wiphy: The kernel sends devices to the \"down\" state before deleting them. The down transition callback calls virt_wifi_cancel_scan, which guarantees putting the device into a not-scanning state as described above.\n\ncfg80211_scan_done also synchronously deletes the scan request passed in as a parameter, so references to that memory should be cleared afterwards.\nhttps://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/latest/source/include/net/cfg80211.h#L2893\n\"... the scan request is given the driver, and will be valid until passed to cfg80211_scan_done().\"",
      "parentUuid": "4a1d387a_6b994122",
      "revId": "9222f683679b26ff23788e888706451c3b2caafa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "edfbdb9a_be9babe6",
        "filename": "drivers/staging/android/virt_wifi.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1381006
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-15T00:17:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are you saying cfg80211_scan_done is idempotent?  The main thing that stuck out to me is potentially hitting multiple calls to cfg80211_scan_done.",
      "parentUuid": "60148d39_4119f5a4",
      "revId": "9222f683679b26ff23788e888706451c3b2caafa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b510819b_8c52fd7e",
        "filename": "drivers/staging/android/virt_wifi.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291337
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-15T00:54:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "cfg80211_scan_done is definitely not idempotent, one side-effect includes a free of the passed in scan request.\n\nThe whole construction with cancel_delayed_work_sync is to enforce there is exactly one call to cfg80211_scan_done, through depending on cancel_delayed_work_sync\u0027s return value indicating whether or not it canceled any outstanding work. The specification is that it never interrupts the work partway through execution; cancel_delayed_work_sync cancels the work if it hasn\u0027t started or blocks until the executing work function is done.\n\nhttps://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v4.20-rc2/source/kernel/workqueue.c#L3126",
      "parentUuid": "edfbdb9a_be9babe6",
      "revId": "9222f683679b26ff23788e888706451c3b2caafa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8bdfe7c9_81cf118c",
        "filename": "drivers/staging/android/virt_wifi.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1381006
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-15T07:49:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It seems like the following series of events is possible then (correct me if wrong):\n\n\t- 186: cfg80211_scan_done(priv-\u003escan_request, false);\n\t- 194: cancel_delayed_work_sync(\u0026priv-\u003escan_result);\n\t- 195: if (priv-\u003escan_request) {\n\t- 187: priv-\u003escan_request \u003d NULL;\n\t- 196:   cfg80211_scan_done(priv-\u003escan_request, true);\n\nI believe this is the only path I see that I\u0027m concerned about, at a glance.",
      "parentUuid": "b510819b_8c52fd7e",
      "revId": "9222f683679b26ff23788e888706451c3b2caafa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "36ab9a63_fd08e96d",
        "filename": "drivers/staging/android/virt_wifi.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291337
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-15T21:38:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My understanding of the specification of cancel_delayed_work_sync is it treats the virt_wifi_scan_result function as atomic. After cancel_delayed_work_sync returns, virt_wifi_scan_result will not be running. It either cancels the operation if it hasn\u0027t started or blocks until the operation is done, or does nothing if nothing is scheduled. The return value of cancel_delayed_work_sync is whether there was pending work or not.\n\nI could also be misreading the specification of cancel_delayed_work_sync though, in which case there are probably more issues with how the async stuff interacts here.",
      "parentUuid": "8bdfe7c9_81cf118c",
      "revId": "9222f683679b26ff23788e888706451c3b2caafa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
